# `for (let place = 0; place < rows.length; place++) total = total + weights[place]`, both constants of the file of which nothing is known: the loop only MEASURES `rows`. Read in every turn off a value that may be anything, the length may run other code, so the typed turn left at the head, every time:
# 1,320 instructions a call for 16 turns, where the same loop bound by `weights.length` takes 183.
# A holder that a loop only measures is expected to be a list and asked in front of it, but only a holder that stands in front of the loop was looked at, and a variable of the file is read in the turn. Every turn passes the head, so the question may stand in front.
# Now the variable is read in front of the loop for its length too, and the name under which its value enters is the holder that is asked.
# Each read of a variable of the file is a value of its own, so "only measured" is asked of the variable over the whole body: in `alsoRead` the body reads `table[0]` as well, and nothing is asked of `table`.
